        "patch_notes": "Root cause: StaticHandlerImpl.handle() computed the served path as HttpUtils.removeDots(URIDecoder.decodeURIComponent(context.normalisedPath(), false)). Neither normalizePath nor removeDots treats a backslash ('\\\\') as a path separator, so a request-controlled backslash traversal payload (e.g. %5c..%5c) survived normalization and flowed unsanitized through getFile()/Utils.pathOffset() into the Vert.x FileSystem lookup, enabling directory traversal on platforms where '\\\\' is a separator (Windows).\n\nFix: Insert `.replace('\\\\', '/')` on the decoded path before HttpUtils.removeDots(...), so backslashes are treated as path separators and collapsed by dot-segment removal along with '/'. This is the exact approach used in the upstream vertx-web 3.9.4 fix. A single-line source change in the one identified sink; no behavioral change for legitimate '/'-based paths.\n\nRegression test: Added StaticHandlerTest.testBackslashTraversalNormalized which issues GET /somedir/..%5c..%5cotherpage.html. With the fix the backslashes are treated as separators and the path collapses inside the web root to /otherpage.html (HTTP 200, \"Other page\" body). Without the fix the literal name \"somedir/..\\\\..\\\\otherpage.html\" is looked up and does not exist, so the assertion fails — making the test a precise, platform-independent discriminator that locks in the fix. Note: the install step in regression_commands is required because building/testing vertx-web needs the sibling vertx-web-common reactor module installed to the local .m2 first.\n\nVerification: The provided POV (bash .security-pipeline/pov/run_pov.sh) was VULNERABLE (200, secret served) before and is now blocked (404, exit code 1) after the patch. Full StaticHandlerTest suite: 64 tests, 0 failures.",
